<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bantu Education Act, 1953</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bantu Education Act of 1953 was a South African apartheid law that placed African schooling under the central government's Native Affairs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> and enforced a separate, deliberately inferior curriculum for black children. Most mission schools, which had educated many African leaders, were closed or taken over. Opposition to it, and later to the imposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> as a teaching language, fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soweto-uprising">Soweto uprising</a> of 1976.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bantu Education</b> gave African children deliberately inferior schooling.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act of 1949 and the Immorality Act banned marriage and sex across the colour line; the Suppression of Communism Act of 1950 gave the state wide powers against its opponents; and the <b>Bantu Education Act, 1953</b>, imposed an inferior schooling on African children.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
